232:
The purpose of this code of ethics is to:
(1) Encourage high ethical standards in official conduct by city officials;
(2) Establish guidelines for ethical standards of conduct for all such officials by setting forth those acts or actions that are incompatible with the interest of the city;
(3) Require disclosure by such officials of private financial or other interest in matters affecting the city; and
(4) Serve as a basis for disciplining those who refuse to abide by its terms.
(Ord. No. 12-02, § A, 4-9-2012)

310:
All licenses issued under this division shall:
(1) Permit the licensee to sell the beverage or beverages for which the license is issued solely within the City of LaFayette, Georgia, pursuant to the terms of this division and consistent with the laws of the State of Georgia and the United States;
(2) Expire on December 31 of each year and an application for renewal shall be made and filed annually on or before October 31 of each year. Any licensee must annually meet the requirements set forth by the city in order to obtain a renewal of any license. Any licensee making proper application with supporting documents for a license to operate during the following calendar year, and having filed such application before October 31, shall be permitted to continue to operate pending final approval of the licensee's application for the following year if final approval is not granted before January 1;
(3) Not be transferred from one (1) person to another or from one (1) location to another; and
(4) Permit the licensee to sell malt beverages or wine or combination thereof, depending upon the license issued, for beverage purposes by the drink for consumption only on the premises where sold.
(Ord. No. 2010-02, § 4, 7-12-2010)

339:
(a) No licensee or employee or agent of a licensee, in connection with the sale or other disposition of malt beverages and/or wine for consumption on the premises, shall:
(1) Offer or deliver any free malt beverage or wine to any person or group of persons;
(2) Deliver more than one (1) malt beverage or wine to one (1) person at a time;
(3) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver any person or group of persons any malt beverage or wine at a price less than the price regularly charged for such malt beverage or wine during the same calendar week, except at private functions not open to the public;
(4) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver to any person or group of persons an unlimited number of malt beverages or wine during any set period of time for a fixed price, except at private functions not open to the public;
(5) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver malt beverages or wine to any person or group of persons on any one (1) day at prices less than those charged the general public on that day, except at private functions not open to the public;
(6) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver malt beverages or wine in any container which holds more than thirty-two (32) fluid ounces (.947 liters), except to two (2) or more persons at any one (1) time; or
(7) Encourage or permit on the licensed premises any game or contest which involves the drinking of malt beverages and/or wine or the awarding of malt beverages or wine as a prize.
(b) Each licensee shall maintain a schedule of the price charged for all malt beverage and wine to be served and consumed on the licensed premises or in any room or part thereof. The licensee shall not vary the schedule of prices from day to day or from hour to hour within a single day.
(c) No licensee shall advertise or promote in any way, whether within or without the licensed premises, any of the practices prohibited under subsection (a) of this section.
(d) No provision of this section shall be construed to prohibit licensees from including a malt beverage or wine as part of a meal package, or to prohibit the sale or delivery of wine by the bottle or carafe when sold with meals or to more than one (1) person.
(e) It is the intent of this section to prohibit activities typically associated with promotions referred to as happy hour or similarly designated promotions.
(Ord. No. 2010-02, § 33, 7-12-2010)

370:
(a) No licensee or employee or agent of a licensee, in connection with the sale or other disposition of distilled spirits for consumption on the premises, shall:
(1) Offer or deliver any free alcoholic beverage to any person or group of persons;
(2) Deliver more than one (1) alcoholic beverage to one (1) person at a time;
(3) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver any person or group of persons any alcoholic beverage at a price less than the price regularly charged for such alcoholic beverage during the same calendar week, except at private functions not open to the public;
(4) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver to any person or group of persons an unlimited number of distilled spirits during any set period of time for a fixed price, except at private functions not open to the public;
(5) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver distilled spirits to any person or group of persons on any one (1) day at prices less than those charged the general public on that day, except at private functions not open to the public;
(6) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver distilled spirits in any container which holds more than sixteen (16) fluid ounces, except to two (2) or more persons at any one (1) time; or
(7) Encourage or permit on the licensed premises any game or contest which involves the drinking of distilled spirits or the awarding of distilled spirits as a prize.
(b) Each licensee shall maintain a schedule of the price charged for all distilled spirits to be served and consumed on the licensed premises or in any room or part thereof. The licensee shall not vary the schedule of prices from day to day or from hour to hour within a single day.
(c) No licensee shall advertise or promote in any way, whether within or without the licensed premises, any of the practices prohibited under subsection (a) of this section.
(d) No provision of this section shall be construed to prohibit licensees from including an alcoholic beverage as part of a meal package, or to prohibit the sale or delivery of wine by the bottle or carafe when sold with meals or to more than one (1) person.
(e) It is the intent of this section to prohibit activities typically associated with promotions referred to as happy hour or similarly designated promotions.
(Ord. No. 18-10, § 3, 12-10-2018)

417:
The owner or keeper of a vicious or potentially dangerous animal must report to the city's animal control department within twenty-four (24) hours of any of the following events:
(1) Escape of the animal;
(2) An attack on a human or domestic animal by the vicious animal;
(3) Transfer of location of the animal;
(4) Transfer of ownership of the animal; or
(5) Death of the animal.
(Ord. No. 07/8, §§ 4, 5, 9-17-2007; Ord. No. 22/01, § 20, 1-10-2022)
